## Resumo das Decisões de Design

| Decisão | Escolha | Justificativa |
|---------|---------|---------------|
| Storage | Array em memória | Simplicidade educacional, fácil substituição por DB |
| IDs | Auto-incremento numérico | Legibilidade e facilidade de teste |
| Async | async/await + try/catch | Legibilidade vs callbacks aninhados |
| Erros | Classes personalizadas com statusCode | Diferenciação clara no middleware global |
| Roteamento | express.Router() | Modularidade e reusabilidade |
| Validação | Middleware antes dos handlers | Separação de responsabilidades |
| Respostas | `{ data: ... }` wrapper | Consistência entre sucesso e erro |
| DELETE | 204 No Content | Convenção REST para recursos excluídos |

# REST API for Book Management — Full Educational Implementation

## Overview

This project implements a complete CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) REST API for managing books using **Node.js** and **Express.js**. We choose Express because it is the most widely-used Node.js web framework, offering a minimal yet powerful routing layer, middleware pipeline, and ecosystem. The API follows standard HTTP conventions: `GET` for retrieval, `POST` for creation, `PUT` for full updates, `DELETE` for removal, and uses proper status codes (`200`, `201`, `400`, `404`, `500`). All data is stored in-memory (an array of objects) so the API works immediately without a database — ideal for learning. Every function includes JSDoc comments, custom error classes ensure predictable error handling, and middleware validates input before it reaches route handlers. Below you'll find each file with detailed explanations between sections.

